hadiths[8]{hadithnumber,text}:
"334","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that I am astonished at the person who fasts before the beginning of the month of Ramadan, even though the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When you see the crescent, then fast, and when you see (the crescent of Eid), then break your fast. If the sky is cloudy, then complete the count to thirty."
"335","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you see the moon, then fast, and when you see the moon, then break the fast. If the sky is cloudy, then complete the count to thirty."" The narrator says that Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) would fast one day before seeing the moon. Ibrahim (may Allah have mercy on him) was asked if he also fasted one day before, and he said, ""Yes."
"336","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not fast a day or two before Ramadan (as a means of welcoming it), except for a person who has a habit of fasting (such as on Thursdays, Mondays, or every other day), then if that day coincides with the days just before Ramadan, he may fast."
"337","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) mentioned Ramadan and said: ""Do not fast before sighting the moon, and do not break the fast before sighting the moon. If the sky is cloudy, then complete thirty days."
"338","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The month can also be of twenty-nine days. Do not fast until you see the moon, and do not stop fasting until you see the moon. If it is cloudy, then complete the count to thirty."
"339","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Do not fast one or two days before the month of Ramadan, except for someone who has a habitual fast and it coincides with that day, then he may fast. Fast upon sighting the crescent moon, and break your fast upon sighting the crescent moon. If the sky is cloudy, then complete the count to thirty, then celebrate Eid."
"340","Umar bin Salim Al-Zurqi (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates from his mother, she says: We were in Mina when Sayyiduna Ali bin Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) was riding a camel and proclaiming: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""These are days of eating and drinking; no one should fast during them."" He was announcing this loudly while riding the camel behind the people."
"341","Abu Murrah (may Allah have mercy on him), the freed slave of Amr bin Al-As (may Allah be pleased with him), narrates: He and Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) went to Amr bin Al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) two days after Eid al-Adha. Sayyiduna Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) presented food, so Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said, ""I am fasting."" Sayyiduna Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Break your fast, for in these days the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us to break the fast and forbade fasting on these days."" Abu Murrah (may Allah have mercy on him) says that Sayyiduna Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) broke his fast, and both of them ate the food, and I ate with them."
